{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1789487355,"nonce":"y8HIEcC798EglK7XxVmQza7iOxo=","title":"Dict lookups sit in the equality cycle, and every probe compares through zb_any_eq","message":"`zb_dict_eq` looks keys up with `zb_dict_find`, `zb_dict_find_hashed` compares with `zb_any_eq`, and `zb_any_eq` compares dicts with `zb_dict_eq`: one call cycle, so under the inliner's rule for re-entrant cycles no lookup ever inlined into `zb_dict_get_default`, `zb_dict_set` or a program. And every probe of two string keys went `zb_any_eq` -\u003e `zb_str_eq` -\u003e `$String$equals`.\n\nNow equality has its own copies of the two lookups (`zb_dict_find_eq`, `zb_dict_find_hashed_eq`, generated from the same definition), so the ordinary ones are outside the cycle; a probe tries the key's address, then, for two strings (the looked-up key's category read once before the loop), `zb_str_eq` directly, else `zb_any_eq`. `string_char_count` in the string plugin counts non-continuation bytes rather than validating and iterating chars.\n\nMeasured on the M1 Pro, exec ms without the collector on 300 k lookups: get 39 to 34, set 37 to 33, the counting loop `counts[tok] = counts.get(tok, 0) + 1` 51 to 46.","files":null}]}